WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.365
Test-first prompting.

2
00:00:01.645 --> 00:00:09.304
The most reliable loop with an agent is the old test-driven one, done deliberately: State the behavior and write the test first.

3
00:00:09.584 --> 00:00:14.811
Either write it yourself or ask the agent to write only the test, from your spec, and stop.

4
00:00:15.091 --> 00:00:23.987
The docs’ own example prompt for a bug is: describe the symptom, point at the likely area, “write a failing test that reproduces it, then fix it.

5
00:00:24.267 --> 00:00:24.564
”.

6
00:00:24.844 --> 00:00:26.935
Confirm it fails, for the right reason.

7
00:00:27.215 --> 00:00:27.940
Run it.

8
00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:31.932
A new test that passes before any fix is testing the wrong thing.

9
00:00:32.212 --> 00:00:36.372
Read the failure message: it should describe the bug, not an import error.

10
00:00:36.652 --> 00:00:38.146
Review and commit the test.

11
00:00:38.426 --> 00:00:39.770
This is the contract.

12
00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:47.815
Reading a 15-line test is far easier than reading the 150-line fix, and it’s where your judgment adds the most.

13
00:00:48.095 --> 00:00:50.826
Ask for the implementation, with the tests fixed.

14
00:00:51.106 --> 00:00:54.882
“Make this test pass without modifying any file under tests/.

15
00:00:55.162 --> 00:00:58.234
If you believe a test is wrong, stop and tell me why.

16
00:00:58.514 --> 00:00:58.811
”.

17
00:00:59.091 --> 00:01:00.350
Require evidence.

18
00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:03.830
“Run python3 -m pytest -q and show the output.

19
00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:11.107
” The docs suggest asking for the command it ran and its output, because “all tests pass” on its own tells you very little.

20
00:01:11.387 --> 00:01:21.862
Committing the test before the fix has a second benefit: the fix’s diff can’t hide a change to the test, because any test change shows up separately against a commit you reviewed.
